# Build context exclusions. The .env block is the load-bearing one; the rest
# keeps host-local state and caches out of the image.
.git
.venv
.local
.worktrees
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.hypothesis
**/*.egg-info
.claude
logs
dist
.coverage
docs/screenshots

# Database dumps: 41 MB carrying users.password_hash and api_keys.key_hash.
# beacon.dump is a directory now; the bare name matches it, the globs catch a
# dump dropped loose in the tree. Kept in sync with .gitignore, and neither
# file's rules can be complete -- nothing here produces dumps, so the names
# are typed by hand.
**/beacon.dump
**/*.dump
**/*.dump.*
**/*.backup
**/*.sql.gz

# .env is the dangerous one, and it is worse than a leak.
#
# It holds a live BEACON_JUDGE_API_KEY and REPO_GUARD_NAME_PATTERNS. `COPY . .`
# would bake both into the image pushed to ECR, and they would not sit there
# inert: api/config.py sets `env_file=".env"` and this image's WORKDIR is
# /app, so the DEPLOYED container would read it and drive the LLM judge
# against the workstation's endpoint on that key.
#
# `**/` and not a bare name: .gitignore's `.env` matches at ANY depth, so a
# file at packages/beacon_ui/.env is invisible to git. A root-anchored rule
# here would let exactly that file into the image -- gitignored, so no commit
# ever shows it, and unexcluded, so every build carries it.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key
